2. What is <T> ?

This is a generic type parameter in TypeScript.

fetchJSON<T>

means:

“This function can return ANY type depending on what the caller specifies.”

Think of T as:

a placeholder type
a variable for types


Example:

const data = await fetchJSON<User>(url);

T = User

Promise<User>


3. Why Promise<T> ?

The function is async.

All async functions return a Promise.
Promise<T>

“This function eventually returns a value of type T.”




4. Understanding this line deeply
const options: RequestInit & { next?: { revalidate?: number } }

This is the most advanced part.


RequestInit is a built-in TypeScript type for the fetch() options object.




Example:


fetch(url, {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    Authorization: 'token'
  },
  cache: 'no-store'
})


All these properties are defined inside RequestInit.



Common RequestInit properties


type RequestInit = {
  method?: string;
  headers?: HeadersInit;
  body?: BodyInit;
  cache?: RequestCache;
  credentials?: RequestCredentials;
  mode?: RequestMode;
}


const options: RequestInit = {
  cache: 'no-store'
};


6. What does & mean here?
RequestInit & { next?: { revalidate?: number } }

The & means:

combine two types together

This is called an intersection type.


type A = { name: string };
type B = { age: number };

type C = A & B;

C = {
  name: string;
  age: number;
}

7. So what type is being created here?

RequestInit & {
  next?: {
    revalidate?: number
  }
}

The object:

has all normal fetch options
PLUS an optional next property



8. Why add next?

Because Next.js extends the standard fetch() API.

It supports:

next: {
  revalidate: number
}



This controls ISR (Incremental Static Regeneration).

 {
   cache: 'force-cache',
   next: {
     revalidate: 60
   }
 }

 Cache this request and refresh every 60 seconds.



9. Understanding the ?
next?: { revalidate?: number }

The ? means optional.


IF revalidateSeconds exists:

{
  cache: 'force-cache',
  next: {
    revalidate: revalidateSeconds
  }
}


ELSE:

{
  cache: 'no-store'
}



Case 1 — Revalidation enabled
{
  cache: 'force-cache',
  next: { revalidate: 60 }
}

Meaning:

use cache
regenerate every 60 seconds

Good for:

blogs
product pages
dashboards


In Next.js, this:

next: { revalidate: 60 }

does NOT mean:

“call the API every 60 seconds automatically”

Instead it means:

“Keep cached data for up to 60 seconds. After that, the NEXT request triggers a background refresh.”


This is called stale-while-revalidate behavior.


Timeline Example

Suppose this runs at:

fetch(url, {
  cache: 'force-cache',
  next: { revalidate: 60 }
})
First request (0s)

User visits page.

No cache exists.

So:

API is called
response is cached
Second request (20s)

Another user visits.

Since cache is still fresh (< 60s):

NO API call
cached data returned instantly
Third request (70s)

Now cache is expired.

Next.js does:

Serve OLD cached data immediately
Trigger background API fetch
Update cache with fresh data

So the user usually still gets fast response.


Request comes in
    ↓
Check cache age
    ↓
Fresh? → serve cache
Expired? → serve stale cache + refresh in background



Difference from Polling
Polling
Every 60 seconds:
    call API automatically

This happens even if nobody visits.


Revalidation
Only refresh when someone requests after expiry


Why cache: 'force-cache' ?

This tells Next.js:

Store this fetch result in cache

Without it:

cache: 'no-store'

means:

Never cache
Always fetch fresh data


fetch(url, {
  next: { revalidate: 60 }
})

is mainly a server-side cache in Next.js.


If I stay on the page for 60 seconds, will it auto-refresh?

NO.

Nothing happens automatically.

The page does NOT refresh itself.

The API is NOT called automatically.

The cache simply becomes "expired".


Suppose:

revalidate: 60
t = 0 seconds

You open website.

Next.js:

Calls API
Stores response in server cache

You see data:

Products: Apple, Orange
t = 30 seconds

You are still on page.

Nothing happens.

No API call.

No refresh.

t = 61 seconds

Still nothing happens automatically.

The cache is now simply marked:

STALE (expired)

But page still doesn't reload.



What happens IF you refresh now?

Now this is important.

When you refresh at 61 seconds:

Next.js sees:

Cache expired

Then usually:

It serves old cached data immediately
Starts background revalidation fetch
Updates cache

because stale cache served first.

Meanwhile Next.js fetches fresh API.


Important: Different users/devices

You asked:

Another user is on another device/browser, shouldn’t they get fresh request?

Not necessarily.

Because the cache is often shared at the server/CDN level.


User A visits

API called.

Cache stored.

User B visits 10 seconds later

No API call.

They receive same cached response.

Even from another:

device
browser
country sometimes (via CDN)



Why is this done?

Performance.

Instead of:

1000 users = 1000 API calls

you get:

1000 users = maybe 1 API call


What if I WANT fresh data every refresh?

Then use:

cache: 'no-store'

Now EVERY request:

every refresh
every user
every visit

calls API again.



Quick Comparison
Option	API called when?
no-store	Every request
revalidate: 60	After cache expires AND a new request comes
force-cache	Only first time, then forever cached



